Jim Miller <yohocoma@yahoo.com> writes:

> Of course, even accepting this explanation still leaves a lot of
> nonsense and inconsistency in nethack's conceptualization of keys and
> locks.
> 
> 1.  Everyone who has a key has only a skeleton key; door-specific
> keys, though assumed, don't seem to exist in the dungeon.

Earlier versions are said to have different keys, which lead 
to unpleasant side--effects.  Having played games like Ultima 
VII, I can confirm that managing different keys can be extremely 
annoying, so I'm content with this inconsistency -- if it is 
one.  I rather think that Rodney saw a discount offer of locks 
and lock-equipped chests, and ordered a bunch without reading 
the fine print "non--individual keylocks".  Or even that the 
nethack "world" is such that keylocks simply are more or less 
symbolically and no real protection (hey, if a weaklish Str 8 
wizard can kick any door in, given enough time, what's the use 
of a strong, good lock anyway?).  

> 2.  Disparate and competing figures in the dungeon somehow carry on
> their economic or other lives, living with the fact that they share
> skeleton-key access to each other's private places.  Shopkeepers have
> skeleton keys - they can get into any other shopkeeper's shop.  

And that's exactly the reason why they leave their shops only 
in cases of emergency.  

> The
> gnome king's wine cellars, every locked box and chest somebody left in
> the dungeon, rooms in the castle, rooms in the quests, and on and on -
> the owners of all these things manage an unprecedented level of trust,
> for such a brutal environment.  

Err, leaving a chest unguarded in the middle of a dungeon is in and 
of itself incredibly stupid, with or without locks.  But if you want 
to stop suspension of disbelief, the first thing that comes to my mind 
are the questions 

(a) Why on earth are all these guys and monsters running around 
    in a dungeon?  What are they doing, anyway?  Waiting for a bus?  

(b) If the amulet should be protected so well, why on earth 
    is the entrance to the whole dungeon so poorly guarded?  
    Some traps with poisoned spikes, some rolling bould traps, 
    some rock traps, and you'd barely need the black dragon 
    on duty in the next room.  

(c) What are they eating, drinking, where are they sleeping, who 
    is paying the soldiers, who is raising the kids or whatever 
    you call young orcs, where are their toilets, is Rodney really 
    running around without wearing a robe, what kind of gods are 
    they that they care how long I wait until I pray again, but that 
    they don't care whether my dwarven valk got this watchman's 
    corpse from (once got Mjollnir as a result of accidental 
    watchman slaughtering and sacrificing), why is my gnomish 
    healer a "murderer" when he tackles down some of those obnoxious 
    bigfoot monsters, but not when he slaughters his relatives in 
    the mines?  

> I could go on.  It's best not to hold the game to too rigorous a level
> of consistency, but I also have to argue against those who try to make
> up a backstory that legitimizes every quirk of the game.  

That's kind of a game here, not to be taken too seriously.  

> It gets too stultified and constructed.

Well, the story starts with Rodney, who did not construct one 
level filled entirely with death-traps and dragons to protect 
the amulet, but a whole dungeon filled with extremely useful 
gear and plenty of possibilities to get experience and skills, 
not to mention the altars and priests he conveniently put in for 
the weary adventurers and the shops he allowed for better equipment 
possibilities.  It's hard to get more stultified and constructed 
than that.  

